            <p>Most rehearsal rooms include amplifiers, a full drum kit, a PA system, and microphones as part of the room hire. Musicians need to bring their own instrument, cables, tuner, and earplugs. This guide covers the standard gear found in rehearsal studios, what you need to supply yourself, and a room-by-room breakdown of what Thunder Studio provides across its three spaces.</p>

            <h2>Standard equipment in a rehearsal room</h2>
            <p>A well-equipped rehearsal studio typically provides:</p>

            <p><strong>Guitar and bass amplifiers</strong><br />
            Amps are the core of any rehearsal room. A large room like <a href={`/${lang}/rooms/large-room`}>Studio 1</a> usually has two separate amps — one for guitar, one for bass. Marshall amps are the industry standard: they deliver warm, responsive tone, handle high volumes cleanly, and survive years of heavy use. Smaller rooms may have a single combo amp suitable for guitar or keyboards.</p>

            <p><strong>Full drum kit</strong><br />
            Because drums can't realistically be transported, every serious rehearsal room provides a complete kit. A standard setup includes a bass drum with pedal, snare drum, hi-hat with stand and pedal, crash and ride cymbals, and at least two toms. Drummers can adjust hi-hat height, snare tension, and cymbal angles to match their playing style.</p>

            <p><strong>PA system</strong><br />
            The PA consists of a mixer and a pair of speaker cabinets. It handles microphone signals and any line-level sources like keyboards or backing tracks. Better-equipped rooms add monitor speakers so vocalists can hear themselves clearly without relying on the main PA.</p>

            <p><strong>Microphones with stands</strong><br />
            Rehearsal rooms typically stock dynamic microphones (Shure SM58 or equivalent) — rugged, feedback-resistant, and well-suited to loud environments. They come pre-connected with XLR cables running to the PA mixer.</p>

            <h2>What you should bring yourself</h2>
            <p>Studios provide the backbone, but these items are always your responsibility:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Guitar or bass</strong> — studios don't lend instruments. Your instrument comes with you.</li>
              <li><strong>Instrument cables</strong> — bring at least two. One to use, one spare. A dead cable ends rehearsal immediately.</li>
              <li><strong>Tuner</strong> — a phone app works, but a clip-on tuner is far more reliable in a loud room.</li>
              <li><strong>Drumsticks</strong> — drummers must bring their own, plus a spare pair in case one snaps.</li>
              <li><strong>Picks</strong> — bring several. They vanish.</li>
              <li><strong>Earplugs</strong> — rehearsal rooms run at 95-110 dB. Musicians' earplugs (flat-response plugs) reduce volume without killing the tone.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>For a complete pre-session checklist, see <a href={`/${lang}/blog/how-to-prepare-for-band-rehearsal`}>How to prepare for band rehearsal</a>.</p>

