            <h2>How to use the equipment in a rehearsal room</h2>

            <p><strong>Setting up a guitar or bass amp</strong><br />
            Start with gain low, volume at midpoint, and all EQ knobs at noon (flat). From that neutral starting point, add what you need — more bass for a fuller guitar sound, slightly more gain for drive. Never crank the gain before you've heard the room's acoustics. Sudden high-gain signals can cause feedback and damage speakers.</p>

            <p><strong>Adjusting the drum kit</strong><br />
            Every drummer is a different height and has different preferences. Adjust hi-hat stand height, snare position, and cymbal angles before the session starts. Play lightly through the kit first to check that nothing is loose, and get a feel for the kick pedal — spring tension varies between kits and affects your playing significantly.</p>

            <p><strong>Setting up the PA and microphones</strong><br />
            Plug the XLR cable from the mic into the mixer channel, then open the channel. Start with the gain low, turn on the speakers, and raise the fader gradually until you reach the right level. If you hear feedback (a high-pitched squeal), lower the gain or move the microphone further from the speakers.</p>

            <h2>Taking care of studio equipment</h2>
            <p>Rehearsal room gear is shared by many musicians. These are the rules that keep everything working:</p>
            <ul>
              <li><strong>Don't crank amps past safe levels</strong> — amps pushed too hard distort badly and blow speakers. Use the volume you need to hear the band clearly, not maximum.</li>
              <li><strong>Don't rearrange the drum kit without asking</strong> — if you need to move a cymbal stand or reposition the kit, check with staff first. Return everything to its original position before you leave.</li>
              <li><strong>Report broken gear immediately</strong> — if you find a loose cable, a broken connector, or a cracked drumhead, tell the staff. Don't attempt repairs yourself.</li>
              <li><strong>Unplug cables before switching off amps</strong> — disconnect instrument cables from the amp before powering down, and coil cables neatly when done.</li>
              <li><strong>Turn everything off when you're done</strong> — power down amps, PA system, and any monitors before leaving the room.</li>
            </ul>

            <h2>Frequently asked questions about rehearsal room equipment</h2>

            <p><strong>What equipment does a rehearsal room typically include?</strong><br />
            Most rehearsal rooms provide guitar and bass amplifiers, a full drum kit, a PA system, and microphones with stands. The specific gear varies by studio size and tier.</p>

            <p><strong>What should I bring to a rehearsal room?</strong><br />
            Always bring your own instrument (guitar, bass, or keyboard — studios don't lend these), instrument cables, a tuner, drumsticks (if you're the drummer), picks, and earplugs for hearing protection.</p>

            <p><strong>What is the difference between Studio 1 and Studio 2 at Thunder Studio?</strong><br />
            Studio 1 is the large room for 6-10 musicians, with two Marshall amps (guitar and bass), a full drum kit, PA system, and microphones. Studio 2 is the small room for 2-4 people — it has a guitar amp, PA system, and microphones, ideal for solo practice or small groups.</p>

            <p><strong>How do I set up a Marshall amp in a rehearsal room?</strong><br />
            Start with gain low, volume at midpoint, and EQ flat (bass, mid, treble all at 5). Then adjust from that neutral baseline. Never crank gain to maximum without first checking the room's acoustics — it can cause unwanted feedback and damage speakers.</p>

            <p><strong>Do I need earplugs for rehearsal?</strong><br />
            Yes, especially for drummers and anyone standing close to the kit. Rehearsal room volume typically runs 95-110 dB, which can cause cumulative hearing damage. Musicians' earplugs (flat-response plugs) reduce volume without distorting the sound significantly.</p>

            <p><strong>What is the Control Room at Thunder Studio used for?</strong><br />
            The <a href={`/${lang}/rooms/control-room`}>Control Room</a> has a mixing console, DAW workstation, studio monitors, and rack gear. It's designed for recording, mixing, and post-production — different from a rehearsal room which focuses on live band practice.</p>

            <p>Want a broader overview of how rehearsal rooms work? Read <a href={`/${lang}/blog/what-is-a-rehearsal-room`}>What is a music rehearsal room?</a></p>

